---
// GPU containers on the NVIDIA image: CDI, what the boot units do, and how
// doctor checks it. Facts from Containerfile.nvidia phases 0 and 8,
// system_files.nvidia/.../pulsar-gpu-containers.service, check_gpu_containers
// and model_quadlet in cli/pulsar, and the README's examples.
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'On Pulsar’s NVIDIA image, any podman container can ask for the GPU by name. Run local models and CUDA work in containers, with nothing layered onto the host.';

const SMOKE = 'podman run --rm --device nvidia.com/gpu=all registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora nvidia-smi';

const OLLAMA = [
  'podman run -d --device nvidia.com/gpu=all -p 11434:11434 \\',
  '  -v ollama:/root/.ollama docker.io/ollama/ollama',
].join('\n');

const QUADLET = [
  '[Container]',
  'Image=docker.io/ollama/ollama',
  'AddDevice=nvidia.com/gpu=all',
  'PublishPort=127.0.0.1:11434:11434',
  'Volume=ollama:/root/.ollama',
].join('\n');

const DOCTOR = [
  '$ pulsar doctor gpu-containers',
  'ok    gpu-containers CDI spec matches driver 615.71.09',
  '                     podman run --rm --device nvidia.com/gpu=all IMAGE',
].join('\n');

const FINDINGS: [string, string][] = [
  ['no CDI spec, so containers cannot be given the GPU', 'sudo systemctl restart nvidia-cdi-refresh.service, then read journalctl -b -u nvidia-cdi-refresh.service'],
  ['stale CDI spec for driver …', 'Delete the spec it names. It is one somebody wrote by hand into /etc/cdi, and it went stale at the last driver update.'],
  ['CDI spec matches …, but SELinux blocks containers from the GPU', 'systemctl status pulsar-gpu-containers.service: the unit that turns the boolean on did not run.'],
];

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'GPU containers on Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/gpu-containers', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'run', label: 'Give a container the GPU', level: 2 },
  { id: 'how', label: 'What makes it work', level: 2 },
  { id: 'check', label: 'When it does not', level: 2 },
  { id: 'models', label: 'A model for your agents', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="GPU containers"
  fullTitle="GPU containers on Pulsar — CUDA in podman, via CDI"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/gpu-containers"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>GPU containers</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Local models, CUDA, anything that wants the graphics card: it runs in a container, not on the
    host. The NVIDIA image makes the GPU something a container can simply ask for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="run">Give a container the GPU</h2>
  <p>Ask for it by name. If this prints your card, everything below is already working:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="smoke test" code={SMOKE}></arc-code-block>
  <p>Then run the real thing. ollama, for example:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="ollama" code={OLLAMA}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    The image brings the driver and <code>libcuda</code>; the container brings the CUDA runtime.
    Nothing CUDA is ever installed on the host. For something you want
    running every day, write it as a <a href="/docs/where-things-go#services">quadlet</a>; the
    device is one line:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="~/.config/containers/systemd/ollama.container" code={QUADLET}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    NVIDIA image only. The standard image has no NVIDIA driver to hand out; a container there can
    still get a GPU through <code>/dev/dri</code> for Vulkan.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">What makes it work</h2>
  <p>
    Podman speaks CDI, the Container Device Interface: a spec file that lists the device nodes and
    driver libraries behind a name like <code>nvidia.com/gpu=all</code>. Two units keep it right:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>nvidia-cdi-refresh.service</code></strong>, from NVIDIA’s container toolkit,
      writes the spec for the running driver to <code>/var/run/cdi/nvidia.yaml</code> on every boot.{' '}
      <code>/var/run</code> is wiped at shutdown, so that spec can never outlive the driver it
      describes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>pulsar-gpu-containers.service</code></strong> turns on the SELinux boolean{' '}
      <code>container_use_xserver_devices</code> each boot. Without it a container gets the device
      nodes and the libraries, then dies in <code>nvidia-smi</code> with “Failed to initialize NVML:
      Insufficient Permissions”, which reads like a driver bug and is not one. The boolean only lets a
      container use a GPU it was given with <code>--device</code>; a container not given one has
      nothing to open.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Don’t write your own spec to /etc/cdi">
    Plenty of guides say to run <code>nvidia-ctk cdi generate</code> into <code>/etc/cdi</code>. On
    Pulsar that file survives every driver update, names library versions that no longer exist, and
    fails inside the container. The boot unit already does the job, in the place that cannot go
    stale.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="check">When it does not</h2>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar doctor gpu-containers" code={DOCTOR}></arc-code-block>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>doctor says</th><th>Do this</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {FINDINGS.map(([says, fix]) => (
        <tr><td>{says}</td><td>{fix}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="hint">
    No NVIDIA module loaded at all is the <code>nvidia</code> check’s finding, not this one’s:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting#fixes">Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="models">A model for your agents</h2>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar agent model on</code> runs llama.cpp’s own server image as a rootless quadlet, on
    this GPU (CUDA on the NVIDIA image, Vulkan where there is <code>/dev/dri</code>, CPU otherwise),
    listening on <code>127.0.0.1</code> only and behind a key. It is the easy path to a local model
    for opencode and aider: <a href="/docs/agents#model">Coding agents</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
